# NFL-fantasy re-fetchable network/response caches — every `*_cache/` dir under the fantasy artifacts
# is reproducible from its source API (FFC ADP, FantasyPros ECR, ESPN/Sleeper ranks, Sleeper injuries,
# NF-D6/D7 defense & xFP, the NF1 feature pool) → never committed. (Operator-supplied licensed FF/PFF
# competitor files are covered by the *.csv rule under artifacts/.)
quant_sports_intel_models/football/nfl/fantasy/artifacts/*_cache/
# Regenerated NF1/NF1.1/NF1.3 build-output SUMMARIES (re-emitted by `--mode build`; the parquet/csv
# season projections are already covered above).
quant_sports_intel_models/football/nfl/fantasy/artifacts/*_projection_summary_*.json
# NF3 draft-board STAGING output. `export_draft_board_json.py` writes this dir (`_STAGING_OUT`) purely
# as the local staging area before `--publish` uploads to the prod api-cache bucket — the SERVED copy
# is the S3 object, which `app/backend/routers/fantasy.py` reads; nothing reads these files at runtime
# and no test uses them as a fixture. Every board is re-emitted wholesale on each export, so the
# tracked copies were ~3 MB of churn (16 files rewritten per run) with no reviewable diff.
# ⚠️ If a board is ever needed for review, publish it or diff the S3 object — do NOT re-track these.
quant_sports_intel_models/football/nfl/fantasy/artifacts/draft_board_json/
# NF3.2 track-record STAGING output — same reasoning as draft_board_json/ above:
# `export_track_record_json.py`'s local staging dir before `--publish` uploads to S3; the served copy
# is the S3 object `app/backend/routers/fantasy_public.py` reads, nothing reads these locally at
# runtime or in tests, and every season is re-emitted wholesale on each export.
quant_sports_intel_models/football/nfl/fantasy/artifacts/track_record_json/
# E8.1 MLB prospect-board STAGING output — the MLB twin of draft_board_json/ above, and it was
# simply never added when E8.1 shipped the exporter. `export_prospect_board_json.py` writes this dir
# (`DEFAULT_OUT`) purely as the local staging area before `--publish` uploads to the prod api-cache;
# the SERVED copy is the S3 object `app/backend/routers/` reads. Nothing reads these files at runtime
# and no test uses them as a fixture. Every board is re-emitted wholesale on each export, so a tracked
# copy is ~2.2 MB of unreviewable churn per run (the board.json is one line of minified JSON).
# ⚠️ If a board is ever needed for review, publish it or diff the S3 object — do NOT re-track these.
quant_sports_intel_models/baseball/fantasy/artifacts/prospect_board/
ablation_results/**/*.parquet

